) |\n| [Traded as](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticker_symbol \"Ticker symbol\") | * [Nasdaq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq \"Nasdaq\"): [GEN](https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/stocks/gen) * [S&P 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\") component |\n| Industry | [Cybersecurity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_security \"Computer security\") |\n| Founded | March 1, 1982; 44 years ago (1982-03-01) in [Sunnyvale, California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunnyvale,_California \"Sunnyvale, California\"), U.S. |\n| Founder | [Gary Hendrix](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Hendrix \"Gary Hendrix\") |\n| Headquarters | * [Tempe, Arizona](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempe,_Arizona \"Tempe, Arizona\"), U.S. * [Prague](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague \"Prague\"), Czech Republic |\n| Area served | Worldwide |\n| Key people | * [Vincent Pilette](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_Pilette \"Vincent Pilette\") ([CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\"), [chairman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman \"Chairman\")) |\n| Brands | * [Norton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_(software) \"Norton (software)\") * [Avast](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_(software) \"Avast (software)\") * [AVG](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_(software) \"AVG (software)\") * [Avira](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avira_(software) \"Avira (software)\") * [LifeLock](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LifeLock \"LifeLock\") |\n| Revenue | Increase [US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\")5.00 billion (2026) |\n| [Operating income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earnings_before_interest_and_taxes \"Earnings before interest and taxes\") | Increase US$2.12 billion (2026) |\n| [Net income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_income \"Net income\") | Increase US$973 million (2026) |\n| [Total assets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asset \"Asset\") | Increase US$15.6 billion (2026) |\n| [Total equity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equity_(finance) \"Equity (finance)\") | Increase US$2.61 billion (2026) |\n| Owner | [Pavel Baudiš](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavel_Baudiš \"Pavel Baudiš\") (8.1%)[[1]](#cite_note-1) |\n| Number of employees | c. 3,900 (2026) |\n| Website | [www.gendigital.com](http://www.gendigital.com) |\n| **Footnotes** Financials as of April 3, 2026[[update]](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gen_Digital&action=edit).[[2]](#cite_note-2) |\n\n**Gen Digital Inc.** (formerly **Symantec Corporation** and **NortonLifeLock Inc.**) is a multinational software company co-headquartered in [Tempe, Arizona](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempe,_Arizona \"Tempe, Arizona\"), United States, and [Prague](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague \"Prague\"), Czech Republic. The company provides [cybersecurity software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_security_software \"Computer security software\"), [financial technology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_technology \"Financial technology\"), and services.[[3]](#cite_note-3) Gen is a [*Fortune* 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500 \"Fortune 500\") company and a member of the [S&P 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\") stock-market index. It is listed on the [NASDAQ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ \"NASDAQ\") and [Prague Stock Exchange](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague_Stock_Exchange \"Prague Stock Exchange\").[[4]](#cite_note-4) Its portfolio includes [Norton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_(software) \"Norton (software)\"), [Avast](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_(software) \"Avast (software)\"), [LifeLock](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LifeLock \"LifeLock\"), [Avira](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avira_(software) \"Avira (software)\"), [AVG](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_(software) \"AVG (software)\"), [ReputationDefender](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ReputationDefender \"ReputationDefender\"), [MoneyLion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MoneyLion \"MoneyLion\") and [CCleaner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CCleaner \"CCleaner\").[[5]](#cite_note-5)\n\nOn October 9, 2014, Symantec declared it would split into two independent publicly traded companies by the end of 2015. One company would focus on security, the other on [information management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_management \"Information management\"). On January 29, 2016, Symantec sold its information-management subsidiary, named [Veritas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Technologies \"Veritas Technologies\"), and which Symantec had acquired in 2004,[[6]](#cite_note-Bray_2015-6) to [the Carlyle Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Carlyle_Group \"The Carlyle Group\").[[7]](#cite_note-7) On August 8, 2019, [Broadcom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadcom \"Broadcom\") announced they would be acquiring the [Enterprise Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symantec_Security \"Symantec Security\") software division of Symantec for $10.7 billion.[[8]](#cite_note-Novet_2019-8) After the acquisition, Symantec became known as NortonLifeLock.[[9]](#cite_note-9)[[10]](#cite_note-Avalos_2020-10) After completing its merger with [Avast](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast \"Avast\") in September 2022, the company adopted the name Gen Digital.[[11]](#cite_note-Scroxton_2022-11)\n\n## History\n\n### 1982 to 1989\n\nFounded in 1982 by [Gary Hendrix](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Hendrix \"Gary Hendrix\") with a [National Science Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Science_Foundation \"National Science Foundation\") grant, Symantec was originally focused on [artificial intelligence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence \"Artificial intelligence\")-related projects, including a database program.[[12]](#cite_note-Slaughter_2014_p._69-12) Hendrix hired several [Stanford University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanford_University \"Stanford University\") [natural language processing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_language_processing \"Natural language processing\") researchers as the company's first employees.[[12]](#cite_note-Slaughter_2014_p._69-12)\n\nIn 1984, it became clear that the advanced natural language and database system that Symantec had developed could not be ported from DEC minicomputers to the PC.[[13]](#cite_note-13) This left Symantec without a product, but with expertise in natural language database query systems and technology.[[14]](#cite_note-Jones_2014_p._198-14) As a result, later in 1984, Symantec was acquired by another, smaller software startup company, C&E Software, founded by Denis Coleman and [Gordon Eubanks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Eubanks \"Gordon Eubanks\") and headed by Eubanks.[[14]](#cite_note-Jones_2014_p._198-14) C&E Software developed a combined file management and word processing program called [Q&A](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Q&A_(software) \"Q&A (software)\").[[14]](#cite_note-Jones_2014_p._198-14)\n\nThe merged company retained the name Symantec.[[14]](#cite_note-Jones_2014_p._198-14) Eubanks became its chairman, Vern Raburn, the former president of the original Symantec, remained as president of the combined company.[[15]](#cite_note-15) The new Symantec combined the file management and word processing functionality that C&E had planned, and added an advanced Natural Language query system (designed by Gary Hendrix and engineered by Dan Gordon) that set new standards for ease of database query and report generation. The natural language system was named \"The Intelligent Assistant\". Turner chose the name of Q&A for Symantec's flagship product, in large part because the name lent itself to use in a short, easily merchandised logo. With a user interface designed by director of product management, Brett Walter, Q&A was released in November 1985.[[16]](#cite_note-16)\n\nIn 1986, Vern Raburn and Gordon Eubanks swapped roles, and Eubanks became CEO and president of Symantec, while Raburn became its chairman.[[17]](#cite_note-17) After this change, Raburn had little involvement with Symantec, and in a few years, Eubanks added chairmanship to his other roles.[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*] After a slow start for sales of Q&A in the fall of 1985 and spring of 1986, Rod Turner, a Symantec Sr. Executive, signed up a new advertising agency called Elliott/Dickens, embarked on an aggressive new advertising campaign, and came up with the \"Six Pack Program\" in which all Symantec employees, regardless of role, went on the road, training and selling nationwide in the United States. Turner named it Six Pack because employees were to work six days a week, see six dealerships per day, train six sales representatives per store and stay with friends free or at [Motel 6](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motel_6 \"Motel 6\").[[18]](#cite_note-18) Simultaneously, a promotion was run jointly with SofSell (which was Symantec's exclusive wholesale distributor in the United States for the first year that Q&A was on the market). This promotion was very successful in encouraging dealers to try Q&A.\n\nDuring this time, Symantec was advised by its board members Jim Lally and [John Doerr](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Doerr \"John Doerr\") that if it would cut its expenses and grow revenues enough to achieve cash flow break-even, then [Kleiner, Perkins, Caufield & Byers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleiner,_Perkins,_Caufield_&_Byers \"Kleiner, Perkins, Caufield & Byers\") would back the company in raising more [venture capital](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venture_capital \"Venture capital\"). To accomplish this, the management team worked out a salary reduction schedule where the chairman and the CEO would take zero pay, all vice presidents would take a 50% pay cut, and all other employees' pay was cut by 15%. Two employees were laid off. Eubanks also negotiated a sizable rent reduction on the office space the company had leased in the days of the original Symantec. These expense reductions, combined with strong international sales of Q&A, enabled the company to attain break-even.\n\nThe significantly increased traction for Q&A from this re-launch grew Symantec's revenues substantially, along with early success for Q&A in international markets (uniquely a German version was shipped three weeks after the United States version, and it was the first software in the world that supported German Natural Language) following Turner's having emphasized establishing international sales distribution and multiple language versions of Q&A from the initial shipment.\n\nIn 1985, Rod Turner negotiated the publishing agreement with David Whitney for Symantec's second product, which Turner named NoteIt (an annotation utility for [Lotus 1-2-3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotus_1-2-3 \"Lotus 1-2-3\")). It was evident to Turner that NoteIt would confuse the dealer channel if it was launched under the Symantec name because Symantec had built up interest by that stage in Q&A (but not yet shipped it), and because the low price for the utility would not be initially attracted to the dealer channel until demand had been built up. Turner felt that the product should be marketed under a unique brand name.\n\nTurner and [Gordon E. Eubanks Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Eubanks \"Gordon Eubanks\"), then chairman of Symantec, agreed to form a new division of Symantec, and Eubanks delegated the choice of name to Turner. Turner chose the name Turner Hall Publishing, to be a new division of Symantec devoted to publishing third-party software and hardware. The objective of the division was to diversify revenues and accelerate the growth of Symantec. Turner chose the name Turner Hall Publishing, using his last name and that of Dottie Hall (Director of Marketing Communications) to convey the sense of a stable, long-established, company.[[19]](#cite_note-computerworld-19)[[20]](#cite_note-20) Turner Hall Publishing's first offering was Note-It, a notation utility add-in for Lotus 1-2-3, which was developed by David Whitney, and licensed to Symantec.[[21]](#cite_note-21)[[22]](#cite_note-22) Its second product was the Turner Hall Card, which was a 256k RAM, half slot memory card, initially made to inexpensively increase the available memory for Symantec's flagship product, Q&A. The Turner Hall division also marketed the card as a standalone product. Turner Hall's third product, also a 1-2-3 add-in was [SQZ!](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synex_Systems_Corporation#SQZ! \"Synex Systems Corporation\") a Lotus 1-2-3 spreadsheet compression utility developed by Chris Graham [Synex Systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synex_Systems_Corporation \"Synex Systems Corporation\").[[23]](#cite_note-23) In the summer of 1986 Eubanks and Turner recruited Tom Byers from [Digital Research](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Research \"Digital Research\"), to expand the Turner Hall Publishing product family and lead the Turner Hall effort.\n\nBy the winter of 1986–87, the Turner Hall Publishing division had achieved success with NoteIt, the Turner Hall Card and SQZ!. The popularity of these products, while contributing a relatively small portion of revenues to Symantec, conveyed the impression that Symantec was already a diversified company, and indeed, many industry participants were under the impression that Symantec had acquired Turner Hall Publishing. In 1987, Byers recruited Ted Schlein into the Turner Hall Product Group to assist in building the product family and in marketing.\n\nRevenues from Q&A, and Symantec's early launch into the international marketplace, combined with Turner Hall Publishing, generated the market presence and scale that enabled Symantec to make its first merger/acquisition, in February 1987, that of [Breakthrough Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakthrough_Software \"Breakthrough Software\"), maker of the TimeLine project management software for DOS. Because this was the first time that Symantec had acquired a business that had revenues, inventory, and customers, Eubanks chose to change nothing at BreakThrough Software for six months, and the actual merger logistics started in the summer of 1987, with Turner being appointed by Eubanks as general manager of the TimeLine business unit, Turner was made responsible for the successful integration of the company into Symantec and ongoing growth of the business, with P&L. There was a heavy emphasis placed on making the minimum disruption by Eubanks and Turner.\n\nSoon after the acquisition of TimeLine/Breakthrough Software, Eubanks reorganized Symantec, structuring the company around product-centric groups, each having its development, quality assurance, technical support, and product marketing functions, and a general manager with profit and loss responsibility. Sales, finance, and operations were centralized functions that were shared. This structure lent itself well to Symantec's further growth through mergers and acquisitions. Eubanks made Turner general manager of the new TimeLine Product Group, and simultaneously of the Q&A Product Group, and made Tom Byers general manager of the Turner Hall Product Group. Turner continued to build and lead the company's international business and marketing for the whole company.\n\nAt the TimeLine Product Group, Turner drove strong marketing, promotion and sales programs to accelerate momentum. By 1989 this merger was very successful—product group morale was high, TimeLine development continued apace, and the increased sales and marketing efforts applied built the TimeLine into the clear market lead in PC project management software on [DOS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DOS \"DOS\"). Both the Q&A and TimeLine product groups were healthily profitable. The profit stream and merger success set the stage for subsequent merger and acquisition activity by the company, and indeed funded the losses of some of the product groups that were subsequently acquired.[[19]](#cite_note-computerworld-19) In 1989, Eubanks hired John Laing as VP worldwide sales, and Turner transferred the international division to Laing. Eubanks also recruited Bob Dykes to be executive vice president for operations and finance, in anticipation of the upcoming [IPO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Initial_public_offering \"Initial public offering\"). On June 23, 1989, Symantec had its IPO, opening on [NASDAQ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ \"NASDAQ\") as \"SYMC\".[[24]](#cite_note-24)\n\n### 1990 to 1999\n\nIn May 1990, Symantec announced its intent to merge with and acquire [Peter Norton Computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Norton_Computing \"Peter Norton Computing\"), a developer of various utilities for DOS. Turner was appointed as product group manager for the Norton business, and made responsible for the merger, with P&L responsibility. Ted Schlein was made product group manager for the Q&A business.\n\nThe Peter Norton group merger logistical effort began immediately while the companies sought approval for the merger, and in August 1990, Symantec concluded the purchase—by this time the combination of the companies was already complete. Symantec's consumer antivirus and data management utilities are still marketed under the [Norton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_(Symantec) \"Norton (Symantec)\") name. At the time of the merger, Symantec had built upon its Turner Hall Publishing presence in the utility market, by introducing Symantec Antivirus for the Macintosh (SAM), and Symantec Utilities for the Macintosh (SUM). These two products were already market leaders on the Mac, and this success made the Norton merger more strategic. Symantec had already begun the development of a DOS-based antivirus program one year before the merger with Norton. The management team had decided to enter the antivirus market in part because it was felt that the antivirus market entailed a great deal of ongoing work to stay ahead of new viruses. The team felt that Microsoft would be unlikely to find this effort attractive, which would lengthen the viability of the market for Symantec. Turner decided to use the Norton name for obvious reasons, on what became the *Norton Antivirus*, which Turner and the Norton team launched in 1991. At the time of the merger, Norton revenues were approximately 20 to 25% of the combined entity. By 1993, while being led by Turner, Norton product group revenues had grown to approximately 82% of Symantec's total.\n\nAt one time, Symantec was also known for its development tools, particularly the THINK [Pascal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pascal_(programming_language) \"Pascal (programming language)\"), [THINK C](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/THINK_C \"THINK C\"), Symantec [C++](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C++ \"C++\"), Enterprise Developer and [Visual Cafe](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_Cafe \"Visual Cafe\") packages that were popular on the [Macintosh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Macintosh \"Apple Macintosh\") and [IBM PC compatible](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_PC_compatible \"IBM PC compatible\") platforms. These product lines resulted from acquisitions made by the company in the late 1980s and early 1990s. These businesses and the Living Videotext acquisition were consistently unprofitable for Symantec, and these losses diverted expenditures away from both the Q&A for Windows and the TimeLine for Windows development efforts during the critical period from 1988 through 1992. Symantec exited this business in the late-1990s as competitors such as [Metrowerks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metrowerks \"Metrowerks\"), [Microsoft](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft \"Microsoft\") and [Borland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borland \"Borland\") gained significant market share.\n\nIn 1994, Symantec acquired [Central Point Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Point_Software \"Central Point Software\") for around $60 million.[[25]](#cite_note-25)\n\nIn 1996, Symantec was accused of misleading financial statements in violation of [GAAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generally_accepted_accounting_principles \"Generally accepted accounting principles\").[[26]](#cite_note-26)\n\n### 2000 to 2014\n\n[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/PLM12_24_Competition_Porsche.jpg/250px-PLM12_24_Competition_Porsche.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:PLM12_24_Competition_Porsche.jpg)\n\nSymantec sponsored [Porsche 997 GT3 Cup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porsche_911_GT3 \"Porsche 911 GT3\") competing at the [2012 Petit Le Mans](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Petit_Le_Mans \"2012 Petit Le Mans\")\n\nFrom 1999 to April 2009, Symantec was led by CEO [John W. Thompson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_W._Thompson \"John W. Thompson\"), a former VP at IBM. At the time, Thompson was the only African-American leading a major US technology company. He was succeeded in April 2009 by the company's long-time Symantec executive [Enrique Salem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Salem \"Enrique Salem\").[[27]](#cite_note-27) Under Salem, Symantec completed the acquisition of [Verisign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verisign \"Verisign\")'s Certificate Authority business, dramatically increasing their share of that market.\n\nIn 2009, Symantec released a list of the then \"100 dirtiest websites\", which contain the most [malware](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malware \"Malware\") as detected by [Norton Safe Web](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_Safe_Web \"Norton Safe Web\").[[28]](#cite_note-28)[[29]](#cite_note-29)\n\n[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Symantec_Headquarters_Mountain_View.jpg/250px-Symantec_Headquarters_Mountain_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Symantec_Headquarters_Mountain_View.jpg)\n\nFormer Symantec headquarters in [Mountain View, California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_View,_California \"Mountain View, California\")\n\nSalem was abruptly fired in 2012 for disappointing earnings performance and replaced by [Steve Bennett](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Bennett_(software_entrepreneur) \"Steve Bennett (software entrepreneur)\"), a former CEO of Intuit and GE executive.[[30]](#cite_note-30) In January 2013, Bennett announced a major corporate reorganization, with the goal of reducing costs and improving Symantec's product line. He said that sales and marketing \"had been high costs but did not provide quality outcomes\". He concluded that \"Our system is just broken\".[[31]](#cite_note-31)\n\nRobert Enderle of CIO.com reviewed the reorganization and noted that Bennett was following the [General Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Electric \"General Electric\") model of being product-focused instead of customer-focused. He concluded \"Eliminating middle management removes a large number of highly paid employees. This will tactically improve Symantec's bottom line but reduce the skills needed to ensure high-quality products in the long term.\"[[32]](#cite_note-32)\n\nIn March 2014, Symantec fired Steve Bennett as CEO and named [Michael Brown](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Brown_(corporate_executive) \"Michael Brown (corporate executive)\") as interim president and chief executive. Including the interim CEO, Symantec had three CEOs in less than two years.[[33]](#cite_note-33)[[34]](#cite_note-34) On September 25, 2014, Symantec announced the appointment of Brown as its president and CEO.[[35]](#cite_note-35) Brown had been interim president and CEO since March 20, 2014,[[36]](#cite_note-36) and a member of the board of directors since July 2005 following the acquisition of [Veritas Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Software \"Veritas Software\"). Brown had been on the Veritas board since 2003.[[37]](#cite_note-37)\n\n#### 2014 information management business spin-off\n\nMain article: [Veritas Technologies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Technologies \"Veritas Technologies\")\n\nOn October 9, 2014, Symantec declared that the company [would separate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spin-off_(business) \"Spin-off (business)\") into two independent publicly traded companies by the end of 2015.[[38]](#cite_note-38) Symantec will continue to focus on security, while a new company will be established focusing on information management. Symantec confirmed on January 28, 2015, that the [information management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_management \"Information management\") business would be called [Veritas Technologies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Technologies \"Veritas Technologies\"), marking a return of the [Veritas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Software \"Veritas Software\") name.[[39]](#cite_note-39) In August 2015, Symantec agreed to sell Veritas to a private equity group led by [the Carlyle Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Carlyle_Group \"The Carlyle Group\") for $8 billion. The sale was completed by February 2016, turning Veritas into a privately owned company.[[40]](#cite_note-40)\n\n### 2016 to present\n\nIn July 2016, Symantec introduced a product to help carmakers protect connected vehicles against zero-day attacks. The Symantec Anomaly Detection for Automotive is an [IoT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_of_things \"Internet of things\") product for manufacturers and uses machine learning to provide in-vehicle security analytics.[[41]](#cite_note-41) [Greg Clark](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greg_Clark_(businessman) \"Greg Clark (businessman)\") assumed the position of CEO in August 2016.[[42]](#cite_note-42)\n\nIn November 2016, Symantec announced its intent to acquire [identity theft](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_theft \"Identity theft\") protection company [LifeLock](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LifeLock \"LifeLock\") for $2.3 billion.[[43]](#cite_note-43)\n\nIn August 2017, Symantec announced that it had agreed to sell its business unit that verifies the identity of websites to [Thoma Bravo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thoma_Bravo \"Thoma Bravo\"). With this acquisition, Thoma Bravo plans to merge the Symantec business unit with its own web certification company, [DigiCert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DigiCert \"DigiCert\").[[44]](#cite_note-44)\n\nOn January 4, 2018, Symantec and [BT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BT_Group \"BT Group\") (formerly British Telecom) announced their partnership that provides new endpoint security protection.[[45]](#cite_note-45)\n\nIn May 2018, Symantec initiated an internal audit to address concerns raised by a former employee,[[46]](#cite_note-46)[[47]](#cite_note-47) causing it to delay its annual earnings report.[[48]](#cite_note-48)\n\nIn August 2018, Symantec announced that the [hedge fund](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedge_fund \"Hedge fund\") [Starboard Value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starboard_Value \"Starboard Value\") had put forward five nominees to stand for election to the Symantec board of directors at Symantec's 2018 Annual Meeting of Stockholders.[[49]](#cite_note-investor.gendigital.com-49) This followed a [Schedule 13D](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schedule_13D \"Schedule 13D\") [filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/849399/000092189518002357/sc13d06297244_08152018.htm) by Starboard showing that it had accumulated a 5.8% stake in Symantec.[[50]](#cite_note-50) In September 2018, Symantec [announced](https://web.archive.org/web/20180918012250/http://investor.symantec.com/About/Investors/press-releases/press-release-details/2018/Symantec-and-Starboard-Announce-Board-Refreshment-Plan-and-Director-Appointments/default.aspx) that three nominees of Starboard were joining the Symantec board, two with immediate effect (including Starboard Managing Member [Peter Feld](https://www.starboardvalue.com/biographies/peter-feld/)) and one following the 2018 Annual Meeting of Stockholders.[[51]](#cite_note-51)\n\nOn May 9, 2019, Symantec announced that Clark would be stepping down and that board member Rick Hill, previously put forward by Starboard, had been appointed interim president and CEO.[[49]](#cite_note-investor.gendigital.com-49) [Vincent Pilette](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_Pilette \"Vincent Pilette\") also joined Symantec as its new CFO.[[49]](#cite_note-investor.gendigital.com-49)\n\nOn August 8, 2019, [Broadcom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadcom \"Broadcom\") announced they would be acquiring the Enterprise software division of Symantec for $10.7 billion.[[8]](#cite_note-Novet_2019-8) This is after having attempted to purchase the whole company. The Norton family of products will remain in the Symantec portfolio. The sale closed on November 4, 2019, and subsequently, the company adopted the NortonLifeLock name and relocated its headquarters from Mountain View, California to LifeLock's offices in Tempe, Arizona.[[10]](#cite_note-Avalos_2020-10)[[52]](#cite_note-52)\n\nIn 2021, a [crypto-miner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptominer \"Cryptominer\") was added to the [Norton 360](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_360 \"Norton 360\") product, called Norton Crypto. Once activated by the user, Norton Crypto mines [Ethereum (ETH)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethereum \"Ethereum\") using the installed machine's graphics card while idle. The program also creates a secure wallet on the same machine.[[53]](#cite_note-53)[[54]](#cite_note-54) Norton announced it was permanently disabling the feature on September 14, 2022, due to the Ethereum merge.[[55]](#cite_note-55)\n\n[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Praha_Nusle_Pikrtova_1a.jpg/250px-Praha_Nusle_Pikrtova_1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Praha_Nusle_Pikrtova_1a.jpg)\n\nEnterprise Office Center building, Gen Digital co-headquarters in [Prague](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague \"Prague\")\n\nUS-based NortonLifeLock and Avast, a European cybersecurity leader based in Czech Republic founded by [Eduard Kučera](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eduard_Kučera \"Eduard Kučera\") and [Pavel Baudiš](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavel_Baudiš \"Pavel Baudiš\") in 1988[[56]](#cite_note-USA_TODAY_2013-56) which was listed on the [London Stock Exchange](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Stock_Exchange \"London Stock Exchange\") since its 2018 [IPO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Initial_public_offering \"Initial public offering\") there,[[57]](#cite_note-57) merged in a deal announced in July 2021 and completed in September 2022, forming a new multinational company called Gen Digital. This merger created a larger cybersecurity firm with a broader portfolio of brands and products, including Norton, Avast, LifeLock and others. The UK's Competition and Markets Authority approved the merger in September 2022.[[58]](#cite_note-58)\n\nIn December 2024, Gen Digital announced that it had entered into a definitive agreement to acquire MoneyLion.[[59]](#cite_note-59) In April 2025, Gen Digital completed its acquisition of MoneyLion in a $1 billion all-cash deal.[[60]](#cite_note-60)\n\nIn February 2025, Norton's antivirus software added Genie AI functionality for fraud protection.[[61]](#cite_note-61)\n\n## Products\n\n### Norton\n\nMain article: [Norton (software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_(software) \"Norton (software)\")\n\nNorton product line includes [Norton AntiVirus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_AntiVirus \"Norton AntiVirus\"), Norton Small Business, [Norton Family](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_Family \"Norton Family\"), Norton Mobile Security, Norton Online Backup, [Norton 360](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_360 \"Norton 360\"), [Norton Utilities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_Utilities \"Norton Utilities\") and Norton Computer Tune Up.[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*] Norton's line also includes [LifeLock](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LifeLock \"LifeLock\") and [ReputationDefender](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ReputationDefender \"ReputationDefender\").\n\nIn 2012, [PC Tools iAntiVirus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IAntiVirus \"IAntiVirus\") was rebranded as a Norton product under the name iAntiVirus, and released to the [Mac App Store](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mac_App_Store \"Mac App Store\"). Also in 2012, the Norton Partner Portal was relaunched to support sales to consumers throughout the [EMEA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe,_the_Middle_East_and_Africa \"Europe, the Middle East and Africa\").[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\n### Avast\n\nMain article: [Avast (software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_(software) \"Avast (software)\")\n\nAvast product line includes [Avast Antivirus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_Antivirus \"Avast Antivirus\"), Avast Premium Security, [Avast Cleanup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_Cleanup \"Avast Cleanup\"), [Avast Secure Browser](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_Secure_Browser \"Avast Secure Browser\"), and [Avast SecureLine VPN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast_SecureLine_VPN \"Avast SecureLine VPN\"). As of 2017,[[update]](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gen_Digital&action=edit) it is the most popular antivirus vendor on the market and it had the largest market share.[[62]](#cite_note-62)\n\n### AVG\n\nMain article: [AVG (software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_(software) \"AVG (software)\")\n\nAVG product line includes [AVG AntiVirus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_AntiVirus \"AVG AntiVirus\"), [AVG Internet Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_Internet_Security \"AVG Internet Security\"), AVG Secure VPN, [AVG PC TuneUp](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVG_PC_TuneUp \"AVG PC TuneUp\"), and AVG Driver Updater. Previously a publicly company in February 2012, it was acquired by [Avast](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast \"Avast\") in July 2016 for $1.3 billion.[[63]](#cite_note-63)\n\n### Avira\n\nMain article: [Avira (software)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avira_(software) \"Avira (software)\")\n\nAvira product line includes Avira Free Security, [Avira Internet Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avira_Internet_Security \"Avira Internet Security\"), Avira Prime and Avira Phantom VPN.\n\n### Other\n\nOther products offered by Gen Digital include [CCleaner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CCleaner \"CCleaner\"), [Recuva](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recuva \"Recuva\"), [Speccy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speccy \"Speccy\"), [Defraggler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defraggler \"Defraggler\"), [HMA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMA_(VPN) \"HMA (VPN)\"), and [SONAR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SONAR_(Symantec) \"SONAR (Symantec)\").\n\n## Mergers and acquisitions\n\nMain article: [List of mergers and acquisitions by Gen Digital](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_mergers_and_acquisitions_by_Gen_Digital \"List of mergers and acquisitions by Gen Digital\")\n\n### ACT!\n\nIn 1993, Symantec acquired [ACT!](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ACT! \"ACT!\") from [Contact Software International](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contact_Software_International \"Contact Software International\"). Symantec sold ACT! to [SalesLogix](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SalesLogix \"SalesLogix\") in 1999. At the time it was the world's most popular [CRM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRM_software \"CRM software\") application for [Windows](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Windows \"Microsoft Windows\") and [Macintosh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macintosh \"Macintosh\").[[64]](#cite_note-64)\n\n### Veritas\n\nOn December 16, 2004, [Veritas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Software \"Veritas Software\") and Symantec announced their plans for a merger. With Veritas valued at $13.5 billion, it was the largest software industry merger to date.[[65]](#cite_note-65) Symantec's shareholders voted to approve the merger on June 24, 2005; the deal closed successfully on July 2, 2005.[[66]](#cite_note-66) July 5, 2005, was the first day of business for the U.S. offices of the new, combined software company. As a result of this merger, Symantec includes storage- and availability-related products in its portfolio, namely [Veritas File System](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_File_System \"Veritas File System\") (VxFS), [Veritas Volume Manager](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Volume_Manager \"Veritas Volume Manager\") (VxVM), [Veritas Volume Replicator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Volume_Replicator \"Veritas Volume Replicator\") (VVR), [Veritas Cluster Server](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veritas_Cluster_Server \"Veritas Cluster Server\") (VCS), NetBackup (NBU), [Backup Exec](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backup_Exec \"Backup Exec\") (BE) and [Enterprise Vault](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_Vault \"Enterprise Vault\") (EV).[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\nOn January 29, 2016, Symantec sold Veritas to [The Carlyle Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Carlyle_Group \"The Carlyle Group\").[[6]](#cite_note-Bray_2015-6)\n\n### Sygate\n\nOn August 16, 2005, Symantec acquired [Sygate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sygate \"Sygate\"),[[67]](#cite_note-Das_2016-67) a security software firm based in [Fremont](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fremont,_California \"Fremont, California\"), California, with about 200 staff.[[68]](#cite_note-68) As of November 30, 2005, all Sygate personal firewall products were discontinued.[[69]](#cite_note-69)\n\n### Altiris\n\nOn January 29, 2007, Symantec announced plans to acquire [Altiris](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altiris \"Altiris\"),[[70]](#cite_note-Finkle_2007-70) and on April 6, 2007, the acquisition was completed.[[71]](#cite_note-71) Altiris specializes in service-oriented management software that allows organizations to manage [IT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_Technology \"Information Technology\") assets.[[70]](#cite_note-Finkle_2007-70) It also provides software for web services, security and systems management products. Established in 1998, Altiris is headquartered in [Lindon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindon,_Utah \"Lindon, Utah\"), [Utah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah \"Utah\").[[72]](#cite_note-72)\n\n### Vontu\n\nOn November 5, 2007, Symantec announced its acquisition of Vontu, a Data Loss Prevention (DLP) company, for $350 million.[[73]](#cite_note-73)\n\n### Application Performance Management business\n\nOn January 17, 2008, Symantec announced[[74]](#cite_note-74) that it was spinning off its [Application Performance Management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Application_Performance_Management \"Application Performance Management\") (APM) business and the [i3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I3_(window_manager) \"I3 (window manager)\") product line to Vector Capital.[[75]](#cite_note-75) Precise Software Solutions took over development, product management, marketing and sales for the APM business, launching as an independent company on September 17, 2008.[[76]](#cite_note-76)\n\n### PC Tools\n\nOn August 18, 2008, Symantec announced the signing of an agreement to acquire [PC Tools](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PC_Tools_(company) \"PC Tools (company)\"). Under the agreement, PC Tools would maintain separate operations. The financial terms of the acquisition were not disclosed. In May 2013, Symantec announced they were discontinuing the PC Tools line of internet security software.[[77]](#cite_note-77)\n\nIn December 2013, Symantec announced they were discontinuing and retiring the entire PC Tools brand and offering a non-expiring license to PC Tools Performance Toolkit, [PC Tools Registry Mechanic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Registry_Mechanic \"Registry Mechanic\"), PC Tools File Recover and PC Tools Privacy Guardian users with an active subscription as of December 4, 2013.[[78]](#cite_note-78)\n\n### AppStream\n\nOn April 18, 2008, Symantec completed the acquisition of [AppStream](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppStream \"AppStream\"), a nonpublic Palo Alto, California-based provider of endpoint virtualization software. AppStream was acquired to complement Symantec's endpoint management and virtualization portfolio and strategy.[[79]](#cite_note-79)\n\n### MessageLabs\n\nOn October 9, 2008, Symantec announced its intent to acquire Gloucester-based [MessageLabs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MessageLabs \"MessageLabs\") (spun off from Star Internet in 2007) to boost its [Software as a Service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_as_a_Service \"Software as a Service\") (SaaS) business. Symantec purchased the online messaging and Web security provider for about $695 million in cash.[[80]](#cite_note-80) The acquisition closed on November 17, 2008.[[81]](#cite_note-81)\n\n### PGP and GuardianEdge\n\nOn April 29, 2010, Symantec announced its intent to acquire [PGP Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PGP_Corporation \"PGP Corporation\") and GuardianEdge.[[82]](#cite_note-82) The acquisitions closed on June 4, 2010, and provided access to established encryption, key management and technologies to Symantec's customers.[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\n### Verisign authentication\n\nOn May 19, 2010, Symantec signed a definitive agreement to acquire [Verisign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verisign \"Verisign\")'s authentication business unit, which included the [Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) certificate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SSL_certificate \"SSL certificate\"), [Public Key Infrastructure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_Key_Infrastructure \"Public Key Infrastructure\") (PKI), Verisign Trust and [Verisign Identity Protection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verisign_Identity_Protection \"Verisign Identity Protection\") (VIP) authentication services.[[83]](#cite_note-83) The acquisition closed on August 9, 2010. In August 2012, Symantec completed its rebranding of the Verisign SSL Certificate Service by renaming the Verisign Trust Seal the Norton Secured Seal.[[84]](#cite_note-84) Symantec sold the SSL unit to [DigiCert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DigiCert \"DigiCert\") for US$950 million in mid 2017.[[85]](#cite_note-Sayer_2017-85)\n\n### RuleSpace\n\nAcquired on October 10, 2010, RuleSpace is a web categorisation product first developed in 1996.[[86]](#cite_note-86) The categorisation is, automated using what Symantec refers to as the Automated Categorization System (ACS). It is used as the base for [content filtering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_filtering \"Content filtering\") [by many UK ISP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_blocking_in_the_United_Kingdom#Technologies \"Web blocking in the United Kingdom\").[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\n### Clearwell Systems\n\nOn May 19, 2011, Symantec announced the acquisition of Clearwell Systems for approximately $390 million.[[87]](#cite_note-87)\n\n### LiveOffice\n\nOn January 17, 2012, Symantec announced the acquisition of cloud email-archiving company LiveOffice. The acquisition price was $115 million.[[88]](#cite_note-Dignan_2012-88) Last year,[*[ambiguous](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Please_clarify \"Wikipedia:Please clarify\")*] Symantec joined the cloud storage and backup sector with its Enterprise Vault.cloud and Cloud Storage for Enterprise Vault software, in addition to a cloud messaging software, Symantec Instant Messaging Security cloud (IMS.cloud).[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*] Symantec stated that the acquisition would add to its information governance products,[[88]](#cite_note-Dignan_2012-88)[[89]](#cite_note-89) allowing customers to store information on-premises, in Symantec's data centers, or both.\n\n### Odyssey Software\n\nOn March 2, 2012, Symantec completed the acquisition of [Odyssey Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odyssey_Software_(mobile_device_management)?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Odyssey Software (mobile device management) (page does not exist)\"). Odyssey Software's main product was Athena, which was device management software that extended [Microsoft System Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_System_Center \"Microsoft System Center\") software, adding the ability to manage, support and control mobile and embedded devices, such as smartphones and ruggedized handhelds.[[67]](#cite_note-Das_2016-67)[[90]](#cite_note-90)\n\n### Nukona\n\nSymantec completed its acquisition of Nukona, a provider of [Mobile Application Management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_Application_Management \"Mobile Application Management\") (MAM), on April 2, 2012.[[91]](#cite_note-91) The acquisition agreement between Symantec and Nukona was announced on March 20, 2012.[[92]](#cite_note-92)\n\n### NitroDesk\n\nIn May 2014 Symantec acquired NitroDesk, provider of TouchDown, the market-leading third-party EAS mobile application.[[93]](#cite_note-93)\n\n### Blue Coat Systems\n\nOn June 13, 2016, it was announced that Symantec had acquired [Blue Coat Systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Coat_Systems \"Blue Coat Systems\") for $4.65 billion.[[94]](#cite_note-94)\n\n### LifeLock\n\nIn 2017, Symantec acquired [LifeLock](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LifeLock \"LifeLock\"),[[95]](#cite_note-95)[[96]](#cite_note-96) and renamed itself to NortonLifeLock in 2019.\n\n### Avira\n\nNortonLifeLock acquired German security firm [Avira](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avira \"Avira\") in December 2020 for $360 million.[[97]](#cite_note-97)\n\n### Avast\n\nIn August 2021, NortonLifelock agreed to merge with Czech cybersecurity software company [Avast](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avast \"Avast\"). The UK [Competition and Markets Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_and_Markets_Authority \"Competition and Markets Authority\") formally cleared the $8.1 billion merger on September 2, 2022.[[98]](#cite_note-98) The company subsequently adopted the name Gen Digital.[[11]](#cite_note-Scroxton_2022-11)\n\n### MoneyLion\n\nIn December 2024, Gen Digital announced its intent to acquire MoneyLion at a transaction value of approximately $1 billion; the deal closed on April 17, 2025.[[99]](#cite_note-99) With the acquisition, MoneyLion's financial management tools and AI recommendation platform were added to Gen's portfolio.[[100]](#cite_note-100)\n\n## Locations around the world\n\nGen Digital has dual headquarters in [Prague](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague \"Prague\"), [Czech Republic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czech_Republic \"Czech Republic\") ([EU](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union \"European Union\")) and [Tempe, Arizona](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempe,_Arizona \"Tempe, Arizona\") ([USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\")).[[101]](#cite_note-101) It has development centers in India (Pune, Chennai and Bangalore)[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*] and also in the United States (Tempe, Arizona) and the Czech Republic (Prague). Additionally, the company has offices in Dublin, Ireland and London, United Kingdom.[[102]](#cite_note-102)\n\n## Security concerns and controversies\n\n### Restatement\n\nOn August 9, 2004, the company announced that it discovered an error in its calculation of deferred revenue, which represented an accumulated adjustment of $20 million.[[103]](#cite_note-103)[[104]](#cite_note-104)\n\n### Endpoint bug\n\nThe arrival of the year 2010 triggered a bug in Symantec Endpoint. Symantec reported that malware and intrusion protection updates with \"a date greater than December 31, 2009, 11:59 pm [were] considered to be 'out of date.'\" The company created and distributed a workaround for the issue.[[105]](#cite_note-105)\n\n### Scan evasion vulnerability\n\nIn March 2010, it was reported that Symantec AntiVirus and Symantec Client Security were prone to a vulnerability that might allow an attacker to bypass on-demand virus scanning, and permit malicious files to escape detection.[[106]](#cite_note-106)[[107]](#cite_note-107)[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\n### Denial-of-service attack vulnerabilities\n\nIn January 2011, multiple vulnerabilities in Symantec products that could be exploited by a [denial-of-service attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denial-of-service_attack \"Denial-of-service attack\"), and thereby compromise a system, were reported. The products involved were Symantec AntiVirus Corporate Edition Server and Symantec System Center.[[108]](#cite_note-108)\n\nThe November 12, 2012, Vulnerability Bulletin of the [United States Computer Emergency Readiness Team](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Computer_Emergency_Readiness_Team \"United States Computer Emergency Readiness Team\") (US-CERT) reported the following vulnerability for older versions of Symantec's Antivirus system: \"The decomposer engine in Symantec [Endpoint Protection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endpoint_Protection \"Endpoint Protection\") (SEP) 11.0, Symantec Endpoint Protection Small Business Edition 12.0, Symantec AntiVirus Corporate Edition (SAVCE) 10.x, and Symantec Scan Engine (SSE) before 5.2.8 does not properly perform bounds checks of the contents of CAB archives, which allows remote attackers to cause a denial of service (application crash) or possibly execute arbitrary code via a crafted file.\"[[109]](#cite_note-109)\n\nThe problem relates to older versions of the systems and a patch is available. US-CERT rated the seriousness of this vulnerability as a 9.7 on a 10-point scale. The \"decomposer engine\" is a component of the scanning system that opens [containers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_container_format \"Digital container format\"), such as compressed files, so that the scanner can evaluate the files within.[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\n### Scareware lawsuit\n\nIn January 2012, James Gross filed a lawsuit against Symantec for distributing fake [scareware](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scareware \"Scareware\") scanners that purportedly alerted users of issues with their computers. Gross claimed that after the scan, only some of the errors and problems were corrected, and he was prompted by the scanner to purchase a Symantec app to remove the rest. Gross claimed that he bought the app, but it did not speed up his computer or remove the detected viruses. He hired a digital forensics expert to back up this claim. Symantec denied the allegations and said that it would contest the case.[[110]](#cite_note-110) Symantec settled a $11 million fund (up to $9 to more than 1 million eligible customers representing the overpaid amount for the app) and the case was dismissed in court.[[111]](#cite_note-111)[[112]](#cite_note-112)\n\n### Source code theft\n\nOn January 17, 2012, Symantec disclosed that its network had been hacked. A hacker known as \"Yama Tough\" had obtained the [source code](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_code \"Source code\") for some Symantec software by hacking an Indian government server.[[113]](#cite_note-Keizer_2012-113) Yama Tough released parts of the code and threatened to release more. According to Chris Paden, a Symantec spokesman, the source code that was taken was for Enterprise products that were between five and six years old.[[113]](#cite_note-Keizer_2012-113)\n\nOn September 25, 2012, an affiliate of the hacker group [Anonymous](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anonymous_(group) \"Anonymous (group)\") published source code from Norton Utilities.[[114]](#cite_note-Constantin_2012-114) Symantec confirmed that it was part of the code that had been stolen earlier, and that the leak included code for 2006 versions of Norton Utilities, pcAnywhere and Norton Antivirus.[[114]](#cite_note-Constantin_2012-114)\n\n### Verisign data breach\n\nIn February 2012, it was reported that Verisign's network and data had been hacked repeatedly in 2010, but that the breaches had not been disclosed publicly until they were noted in an [SEC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Securities_and_Exchange_Commission \"U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission\") filing in October 2011.[[115]](#cite_note-Menn_2012-115) Verisign did not provide information about whether the breach included its certificate authority business, which was acquired by Symantec in late 2010.[[115]](#cite_note-Menn_2012-115) Oliver Lavery, director of security and research for nCircle, asked rhetorically, \"Can we trust any site using Verisign SSL certificates? Without more clarity, the logical answer is no.\"[[116]](#cite_note-116)[[117]](#cite_note-117)\n\n### pcAnywhere exploit\n\nOn February 17, 2012, details of an exploit of pcAnywhere were posted. The exploit would allow attackers to crash pcAnywhere on computers running [Windows](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Windows \"Microsoft Windows\").[[118]](#cite_note-118) Symantec released a hotfix for the issue twelve days later.[[119]](#cite_note-119)\n\n### Hacking of *The New York Times* network\n\nAccording to [Mandiant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandiant \"Mandiant\"), Symantec security products used by *[The New York Times](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times \"The New York Times\")* detected only one of 45 pieces of malware that were installed by Chinese hackers on the newspaper's network during three months in late 2012.[[120]](#cite_note-120)[[121]](#cite_note-121) Symantec responded:\n\n> \"Advanced attacks like the ones the *New York Times* described in the following article, <<https://www.nytimes.com/2013/01/31/technology/chinese-hackers-infiltrate-new-york-times-computers.html>>, underscore how important it is for companies, countries and consumers to make sure they are using the full capability of security solutions. The advanced capabilities in our [E]ndpoint offerings, including our unique reputation-based technology and behavior-based blocking, specifically target sophisticated attacks. Turning on only the signature-based anti-virus components of [E]ndpoint solutions alone [is] not enough in a world that is changing daily from attacks and threats. We encourage customers to be very aggressive in deploying solutions that offer a combined approach to security. Anti-virus software alone is not enough\".[[122]](#cite_note-122)\n\n### Intellectual Ventures suit\n\nIn February 2015, Symantec was found guilty of two counts of patent infringement in a suit by [Intellectual Ventures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intellectual_Ventures \"Intellectual Ventures\") Inc and ordered to pay $17 million in compensation and damages,[[123]](#cite_note-123) In September 2016, this decision was reversed on appeal by the Federal Circuit.[[124]](#cite_note-124)[[125]](#cite_note-125)\n\n### Sustaining digital certificate security\n\nOn September 18, 2015, Google notified Symantec that the latter issued 23 test certificates for five organizations, including Google and Opera, without the domain owners' knowledge.[[126]](#cite_note-126) Symantec performed another audit and announced that an additional 164 test certificates were mis-issued for 76 domains and 2,458 test certificates were mis-issued for domains that had never been registered. Google requested that Symantec update the public incident report with proven analysis explaining the details on each of the failures.[[127]](#cite_note-127)\n\nThe company was asked to report all the certificates issued to the Certificate Transparency log henceforth.[[128]](#cite_note-Constantin_2015-128)[[129]](#cite_note-129) Symantec has since reported implementing Certificate Transparency for all its SSL Certificates. Above all, Google has insisted that Symantec execute a security audit by a third party and to maintain tamper-proof security audit logs.[[128]](#cite_note-Constantin_2015-128)\n\n### Google and Symantec clash on website security checks\n\nOn March 24, 2017, Google stated that it had lost confidence in Symantec, after the latest incident of improper certificate issuance.[[130]](#cite_note-130)[[131]](#cite_note-131) Google says millions of existing Symantec certificates will become untrusted in Google Chrome over the next 12 months. According to Google, Symantec partners issued at least 30,000 certificates of questionable validity over several years, but Symantec disputes that number.[[132]](#cite_note-132) Google said Symantec failed to comply with industry standards and could not provide audits showing the necessary documentation.[[133]](#cite_note-133)[[134]](#cite_note-134)\n\nGoogle's Ryan Sleevi said that Symantec partnered with other CAs (CrossCert (Korea Electronic Certificate Authority), [Certisign Certificatadora Digital](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Certisign \"Certisign\"), Certsuperior S. de R. L. de C.V., and Certisur S.A.) who did not follow proper verification procedures leading to the misissuance of certificates.[[135]](#cite_note-135)\n\nFollowing discussions in which Google had required that Symantec migrate Symantec-branded certificate issuance operations a non-Symantec-operated \"Managed Partner Infrastructure\",[[136]](#cite_note-136) a deal was announced whereby [DigiCert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DigiCert \"DigiCert\") acquired Symantec's website security business.[[137]](#cite_note-137) In September 2017, Google announced that starting with Chrome 66, \"Chrome will remove trust in Symantec-issued certificates issued prior to June 1, 2016\".[[138]](#cite_note-O'Brien_2017-138) Google further stated that \"by December 1, 2017, Symantec will transition issuance and operation of publicly-trusted certificates to DigiCert infrastructure, and certificates issued from the old Symantec infrastructure after this date will not be trusted in Chrome.\"[[138]](#cite_note-O'Brien_2017-138) Google predicted that toward the end of October 2018, with the release of Chrome 70, the browser would omit all trust in Symantec's old infrastructure and all of the certificates it had issued, affecting most certificates chaining to Symantec roots.[[138]](#cite_note-O'Brien_2017-138) Mozilla Firefox planned to distrust Symantec-issued certificates in Firefox 63 (released on October 23, 2018),[[139]](#cite_note-139) but delivered the change in Firefox 64 (released on December 11, 2018).[[140]](#cite_note-140) Apple has also planned to distrust Symantec root certificates.[[141]](#cite_note-141)[[142]](#cite_note-142) Subsequently, Symantec exited the TLS/SSL segment by selling the SSL unit to [Digicert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digicert \"Digicert\") for $950 million in mid 2017.[[85]](#cite_note-Sayer_2017-85)\n\n### Norton Crypto\n\nOn July 20, 2021, Norton LifeLock released Norton Crypto, which would've mined [Ethereum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethereum \"Ethereum\") in the background in exchange for periodic payments.[[143]](#cite_note-143) This drew criticism from users, as this was installed automatically, and many users reported having difficulty uninstalling the program.[[144]](#cite_note-144)[[145]](#cite_note-145)\n\n### Columbia patent-infringement lawsuit\n\nIn May 2022, [Columbia University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University \"Columbia University\") won $185 million judgement against NortonLifeLock in a patent-infringement lawsuit.[[146]](#cite_note-146) The jury found that Norton willfully infringed the patents related to antivirus fighting malware.[[147]](#cite_note-147)[[148]](#cite_note-148) In March 2026, Gen Digital overturned a $481 million ruling that Columbia University had won against software company Gen Digital for infringing patents related to cybersecurity technology.[[149]](#cite_note-149)\n\n### TCPA class action settlement\n\nIn January 2026, Gen Digital agreed to a $9.95 million settlement to resolve a class action lawsuit alleging that the company placed prerecorded telephone calls regarding LifeLock or Norton accounts to phone numbers provided by customers, but that reached individuals who had no account with either company because the phone numbers had been reassigned. This was alleged to be in violation of the [Telephone Consumer Protection Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_Consumer_Protection_Act \"Telephone Consumer Protection Act\").[[150]](#cite_note-Bagdonas-150) The settlement covers calls made between February 19, 2021, and October 30, 2025. It received preliminary court approval on January 28, 2026, with a final approval hearing scheduled for July 14, 2026.[[150]](#cite_note-Bagdonas-150)\n\n## Logos\n\n* [![1990–1999](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Symantec_Corporation_logo_1990.svg/120px-Symantec_Corporation_logo_1990.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Symantec_Corporation_logo_1990.svg \"1990–1999\")\n* [![2001–2010](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Symantec_Logo.svg/120px-Symantec_Logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Symantec_Logo.svg \"2001–2010\")\n* [![2010–2019](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Symantec_logo10.svg/120px-Symantec_logo10.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Symantec_logo10.svg \"2010–2019\")\n* [![2019–2022](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Logo_NortonLifeLock.svg/120px-Logo_NortonLifeLock.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_NortonLifeLock.svg \"2019–2022\")\n* [![2022–present](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Gen_logo.svg/120px-Gen_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Gen_logo.svg \"2022–present\")\n\n## See also\n\n[![Wikimedia Commons logo](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4a/Commons-logo.svg/40px-Commons-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Commons-logo.svg)\n\nWikimedia Commons has media related to [Gen Digital](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Gen%20Digital \"commons:Category:Gen Digital\").\n\n* [Huawei Symantec](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huawei_Symantec \"Huawei Symantec\"), a joint venture between Huawei and Symantec\n* [Web blocking in the United Kingdom – Technologies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_blocking_in_the_United_Kingdom#Technologies \"Web blocking in the United Kingdom\")\n* Symantec behavior analysis technologies [SONAR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SONAR_(Symantec) \"SONAR (Symantec)\") and [AntiBot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_AntiBot \"Norton AntiBot\")\n* [Symantec Online Backup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symantec_Online_Backup \"Symantec Online Backup\")\n\n[Portals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Contents/Portals \"Wikipedia:Contents/Portals\"):\n\n* ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Industry5.svg/20px-Industry5.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [Companies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:Companies \"Portal:Companies\")\n* ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/